### Verification Checklist

When creating or configuring a project, verify:

- [ ] Port allocated using port-manager
- [ ] `.port-manager.json` exists in project root
- [ ] Project configuration reads from `.port-manager.json`
- [ ] No hardcoded ports in configuration files
- [ ] Scripts use port from `.port-manager.json`
- [ ] README documents port usage
- [ ] Development server uses correct port
- [ ] API server (if applicable) uses correct port

### Common Port Conflicts to Avoid

**DO NOT manually assign these common ports:**
- 3000 (common Node.js default)
- 4200 (Angular default)
- 5173 (Vite default)
- 8080 (common development server)
- 3001, 3002, etc. (common incremental ports)

**INSTEAD: Let port-manager assign available ports.**

### Exception Handling

**If port-manager is not available:**
- Document the port allocation manually
- Plan to migrate to port-manager when available
- Track port assignments to avoid conflicts
- **Do NOT** randomly pick ports

**If port conflict occurs:**
- Use port-manager to check allocation
- Resolve conflict by reallocating port
- Update project configuration
- Document resolution

## Key Principle

**Port Manager is the source of truth. Never decide ports manually. Always use port-manager for port allocation and management.**

## Integration with Other Rules

This rule integrates with:
- **Project Initialization**: Use port-manager during project creation
- **Template Projects**: Templates should include port-manager integration
- **Package Development**: Packages that create projects should use port-manager
- **Development Workflow**: Always check allocated ports before running projects

